WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.530
A basin is low relative to its surroundings, but basins do

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.210
not all form the same way.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:10.330
Continental extension creates normal faults and downdropped blocks.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.150
Coarse debris collects near steep mountain fronts;

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.430
finer sediment and sometimes evaporites accumulate in basin lakes.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:21.450
If rifting reaches seafloor spreading, the former rifted edge can cool

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.990
and subside into a passive continental margin.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:28.960
Compression also makes basins. Thrust sheets pile extra weight onto the

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.360
crust, bending it down in front of the thrust belt.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.140
That is a foreland basin;

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.140
the Persian Gulf is the example here.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.200
A forearc basin occupies a different position, between a volcanic arc

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.680
and its trench. Similar sedimentary lowlands can therefore record very different

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:44.420
tectonic settings.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.430
Strike-slip movement can open a depression at a releasing bend or

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.770
between appropriately arranged fault segments.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.550
This produces a pull-apart basin, rather than a basin at every

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.770
bend. Broad regional subsidence can also result from cooling, movements at

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.010
depth, or changes to denser minerals.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:05.090
These mechanisms operate over scales much larger than a single fault

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:05.310
block.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.260
The Cascade forearc lies between the volcanic arc and offshore subduction

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.040
zone. Southern California contains basins linked to its complicated fault system.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:19.840
The Gulf Coast records rifting followed by passive-margin subsidence and thick

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.280
sediment accumulation. Ancient basins may no longer be obvious depressions:

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.800
their history survives in unusually thick sedimentary sequences.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:32.770
Michigan's bedrock map forms a broad bull's-eye, with younger layers toward

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.870
the center. In cross section, the layers thicken inward.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:39.030
That tells us the basin was subsiding while sediment accumulated.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.090
Its exact origin remains unresolved in the supplied text, with several

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.450
deep processes proposed. Separate the observed geometry from an uncertain causal

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.790
explanation; a useful model does not erase that uncertainty.
